"CPython, look for documentation from your distributor (that is, whoever "
"provided Python to you). If you are the distributor, see :ref:`optional-"
"module-requirements`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../library/curses.rst:31
msgid ""
"Whenever the documentation mentions a *character* it can be specified as an "
"integer, a one-character Unicode string or a one-byte byte string. An "
"integer is the code of a single encoded byte, optionally combined with "
"attributes and a color pair, as returned by :meth:`window.inch`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../library/curses.rst:36
msgid ""
"Whenever the documentation mentions a *character string* it can be specified "
"as a Unicode string or a byte string."
msgstr ""

#: ../../library/curses.rst:41
msgid ""
"Whether curses may be used from several threads depends on the underlying "
"library and how it was built. In many implementations, including the default "
"build of ncurses, the screen state is shared and not thread-safe; since the "
"blocking and refresh methods (such as :meth:`~window.getch` and :meth:"
"`~window.refresh`) release the :term:`GIL`, unsynchronized use from several "
"threads can then crash the interpreter. Serialize the calls."
msgstr ""

